WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:06.009
Ready for some Game of Thrones season seven
spoilers? No? Too bad! Bend the knee and keep

00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:10.179
watching, because Game of Thrones' Season
7 premiere was memorable for many things,

00:00:10.179 --> 00:00:15.070
like Arya Stark's vengeful assault on the
Frey family, Daenerys Targaryen finally reaching

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:20.039
Dragonstone, Euron Greyjoy declaring himself
for Queen Cersei, and Sansa giving Jon Snow

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.760
some serious sass. But despite all that, season
7 already sucks. You know why? Because Joffrey

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:30.300
is not in it. I miss you Joffrey, so much.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:35.850
But that was all a bit overshadowed by the
cameo of Ed Sheeran, whose musical moment

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.010
on the show was unintentionally hilarious.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:43.079
"It's a pretty song. I've never heard it before."
"It's a new one."

00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:47.949
Sheeran's stint as a Lannister soldier might've
earned a roaring social media response, but

00:00:47.949 --> 00:00:52.730
it was just one in a series of celebrity cameos
that have come and go on the show. Here are

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:56.450
some other famous faces who've popped up in
the Realm over the years.

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:58.550
Gary Lightbody

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:03.340
Sheeran was hardly the first musician to appear
on Game of Thrones. In Season 3, Snow Patrol

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:07.860
frontman Gary Lightbody leads a battalion
of House Bolton soldiers in a rendition of

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.550
"The Bear and the Maiden Fair."

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:14.380
In the world of Westeros, this is a folk song
everybody seems to know. But in the real world,

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:19.190
it was necessary to make audiences aware that
the song exists, so that a later episode could

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:23.580
take that title and run with a very literal
interpretation of it.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.260
Roy Dotrice

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:30.400
If you're a longtime fan of fantasy on television,
you probably remember Roy Dotrice as Ron Perlman's

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:35.080
wise father figure on Beauty and the Beast.
But if you're a fan of George R. R. Martin's

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:39.610
A Song of Ice and Fire, you probably know
him as the celebrated reader of the audiobooks.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:43.470
He was personally chosen by Martin after the
two had worked together on Beauty and the

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:48.410
Beast and was originally announced as Grand
Maester Pycelle for the HBO series.

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:53.290
Due to health reasons, he was replaced by
Julian Glover. But fortunately, he was able

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:58.710
to appear in Season 2 as the pyromancer Hallyne.
While he isn't onscreen much, Hallyne plays

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:03.050
a key role in Tyrion's master plan for the
Battle of the Blackwater, so it's a fitting

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:06.230
use of an actor who will forever be linked
to the books.

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:08.479
David Benioff and D.B. Weiss

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:12.700
The House of Black and White, where Arya Stark
receives her training as a mystical assassin,

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:17.780
features an endless hall filled with columns
lined with the faces of the dead — not statues,

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:22.530
but the actual faces that have been removed
and serve as masks for the Faceless Men. Naturally,

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:27.030
the production design staff who made the faces
ended up including people they knew, most

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:32.191
notably show creators Benioff and Weiss, whose
faces occupy adjacent compartments that receive

00:02:32.191 --> 00:02:34.420
a bit of camera time.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:35.490
Mastodon

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:39.890
The American heavy metal band Mastodon didn't
perform music on Game of Thrones, but they

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:45.300
did contribute a song called "White Walker"
to the Catch the Throne Vol. 2 mixtape HBO

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:49.890
released to tie into the show in 2015. So
it wasn't surprising when members of the band

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:53.800
showed up in the episode "Hardhome," in which
they're killed and then resurrected by the

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:58.500
White Walkers. The band can thank Game of
Thrones executive producer D. B. Weiss for

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.800
those collaborative opportunities because
he's apparently a big fan of the group.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.430
George W. Bush

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:08.900
In Game of Thrones' first season, Sansa is
made to look upon her father's severed head

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:13.880
by the cruel new King Joffrey. And while your
eyes may be scarred by the image of poor Lord

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:18.760
Stark, there are tons of other spiked heads
on display alongside him, including one resembling

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:23.610
former U.S. President George W. Bush, which
rests next to Sansa's septa.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:27.410
Benioff and Weiss revealed that they used
the already-existing head prop for the scene

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:31.740
because of their need for heads, not for any
kind of political statement. But once the

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:37.060
word got out, some people found such a violent
depiction of a former president to be disrespectful.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:41.260
This led to official apologies from HBO as
well as Benioff and Weiss.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.010
Sigur Rós

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:48.290
Another memorable musical cameo comes at Joffrey
and Margaery's wedding in Season 4. The Icelandic

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:53.300
post-rock band Sigur Rós shows up with a
strange set of musical instruments to perform

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.730
a droning take on the House Lannister song
"The Rains of Castamere." It did not please

00:03:57.730 --> 00:03:58.730
the king.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:01.200
"Very good, very good, off you go."

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.450
Will Champion

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:08.940
In one of the most chilling episodes of the
entire series, Coldplay drummer Will Champion

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:13.410
joined the cast to bang the drums in the hall
of the Twins before all hell broke loose.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:17.410
He's shown merrily thumping away as Edmure
Tully and his new bride are sent to their

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:21.870
wedding bed, blissfully unaware of the Red
Wedding massacre that's about to go down.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:25.590
"That is a very strange custom."

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.130
Noah Syndergaard

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:31.770
Ed Sheeran wasn't the only familiar face to
make his debut in Season 7. New York Mets

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:36.820
pitcher Noah Syndergaard also made a buzzy
cameo in the new season as one of House Lannister's

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:39.690
foot soldiers who has incredible aim with
spear-throwing.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:43.550
We don't know if his character survived the
ensuing dragon attack, but in light of how

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:48.100
the rest of the army fared, he probably didn't
last long in the skirmish. And considering

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:51.780
the Mets' 2017 season...he's probably used
to it.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:56.010
Thanks for watching! Click the Looper icon
to subscribe to our YouTube channel. Plus

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.270
check out all this cool stuff we know you'll
love, too!

